# EXPECT_RUNTIME_ERROR
#~
A program that dies inside the VM must leave obr with a non-zero exit status.

Execute (core/vm/vm.cpp) catches an exception that escapes the interpreter,
prints ">>> virtual machine: internal error: ... <<<" and returns -1. The
POSIX entry point (core/vm/posix_main.cpp) discarded that result and returned
0, so on Linux and macOS such a program reported success to whatever ran it --
a shell, a Makefile, a CI step -- and only a reader of stderr knew. Windows
returned the -1.

The way in is a float-formatting precision that is not a number:
Runtime->SetProperty("float:string:precision", "not-a-number") and then any
Float->ToString. Float2Str (interpreter.cpp) and the bridge's F2S case
(jit_common.cpp) read the property with std::stoll, which throws
std::invalid_argument, and neither catches it, so the exception reaches
Execute's catch, or under compiled code the bridge's, on every platform and
in every VM mode.

THE TRIGGER IS ITSELF A KNOWN DEFECT. A bad precision should be refused, not
kill the program. Nothing in the VM raises an exception on purpose, so any
fixture for this catch rides on an unguarded one. This file used
"1e999"->ToFloat() until String->ToFloat stopped throwing
std::out_of_range (it answers inf now); whoever guards the precision parse
must give this file another trigger that escapes Execute as a
std::exception, or it exits 0 and fails as # EXPECT_RUNTIME_ERROR -- the
regression runner will say "should have failed at runtime" and
run_vm_flag_tests.py (section 7) will name the missing message. The case
first seen, on macOS, was Runtime->SetProperty("locale", "xx_YY.bogus")
dying with "locale constructed with null"; #774 makes that call survive, so
it is not used here.

The regression runner passes this file only on a non-zero exit WITH output,
which is exactly the fix: the old obr printed the line and exited 0.

With every method compiled (OBJECK_JIT_THRESHOLD=1, --jit=1) the exception is
thrown inside the interpreter callback that the compiled Main made. Until #778
it could not unwind through the JIT'd frame: libc++ terminated the process
before Execute's catch, and this file carried the JIT opt-out marker so the
runner's second pass would not count an abort as its expected error. The
bridge now catches the exception and reports it as Execute does -- the line,
the stack, exit status 1 -- so both passes run this file in their own mode,
and run_vm_flag_tests.py (section 7) checks the status under --jit=1 as well.
~#

class VmErrorExit {
	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		"before"->PrintLine();
		Runtime->SetProperty("float:string:precision", "not-a-number");
		Float->ToString(1.5)->PrintLine();
		"after"->PrintLine();
	}
}
